<title>MEXICO: Mexico expects to win cement US dispute - minister.</title>
<headline>Mexico expects to win cement US dispute - minister.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1996-09-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Mexican government expects to win its dispute over anti-dumping duties applied to Mexican cement in the United States, Minister of Commerce Herminio Blanco told lawmakers Friday.</p>
<p>&quot;We have supplied all the information relating to Mexican firms and expect to win the hearing,&quot; Blanco said.</p>
<p>Blanco gave no details of how long a dispute resolution panel of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT) might take to issue a ruling, but he insisted that Mexican companies still had a chance to overturn the 1992 decision.</p>
<p>In 1992, the U.S. accused Mexican cement companies of dumping their products at below-cost prices and slapped compensatory duties on the industry.</p>
<p>The decision mainly affected Cemex, then the world's biggest cement exporter, whose sales plummeted, especially in the U.S. market.</p>
<p>--Claudia Villegas, Mexico City newsroom, 525 728-9549</p>
